WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:03.430
[ELECTRONIC MUSIC]

00:00:05.400 --> 00:00:06.130
KRIS BRAUN: Hi.

00:00:06.130 --> 00:00:08.100
Welcome to this
session on implementing

00:00:08.100 --> 00:00:11.040
asynchronous
operations in your UI.

00:00:11.040 --> 00:00:14.040
I'm Kris Braun, the
product lead for Workflows,

00:00:14.040 --> 00:00:16.260
and I'll cover why
getting this right

00:00:16.260 --> 00:00:19.200
leads to building
trustworthy applications.

00:00:19.200 --> 00:00:21.720
Then my colleague
Guillaume Lafore

00:00:21.720 --> 00:00:24.840
will share a couple variations
of a helpful pattern

00:00:24.840 --> 00:00:28.830
for implementing asynchronous
operations in your UI using

00:00:28.830 --> 00:00:32.530
Workflows along with Firestore.

00:00:32.530 --> 00:00:38.500
Your app or website exists to do
the things users ask it to do.

00:00:38.500 --> 00:00:41.710
That might be publishing
their brilliant article,

00:00:41.710 --> 00:00:46.570
placing an order, or authorizing
access for a new employee.

00:00:46.570 --> 00:00:50.650
User trust is the most valuable
thing your app can build--

00:00:50.650 --> 00:00:51.880
or lose.

00:00:51.880 --> 00:00:54.430
How often have you
experienced an app

00:00:54.430 --> 00:00:56.830
that is flaky,
unreliable, or simply

00:00:56.830 --> 00:01:00.010
doesn't give you the feedback
you need to know whether it's

00:01:00.010 --> 00:01:01.720
working or not?

00:01:01.720 --> 00:01:03.970
It only takes one or
two bad experiences

00:01:03.970 --> 00:01:06.340
before I stop
trusting an app, which

00:01:06.340 --> 00:01:08.600
leads me to avoid using it.

00:01:08.600 --> 00:01:11.920
On the other hand, many apps
are so reliable that they

00:01:11.920 --> 00:01:14.230
form unconscious habits
where I don't even

00:01:14.230 --> 00:01:17.650
consider them failing to
do the thing that I ask.

00:01:17.650 --> 00:01:20.650
Our goal as developers
is to create apps

00:01:20.650 --> 00:01:23.890
that win that kind of trust.

00:01:23.890 --> 00:01:27.520
The challenge is, many
things an app can do

00:01:27.520 --> 00:01:29.800
may not be instantaneous.

00:01:29.800 --> 00:01:33.730
Processing may take from several
seconds to several hours.

00:01:33.730 --> 00:01:37.330
This might be because of the
complexity of the computation,

00:01:37.330 --> 00:01:40.510
waiting for a limited
resource, or connection

00:01:40.510 --> 00:01:42.970
to a real-world process
that just takes time,

00:01:42.970 --> 00:01:45.040
like 3D printing.

00:01:45.040 --> 00:01:49.120
In addition, many operations
involve multiple services

00:01:49.120 --> 00:01:53.830
performing a chain of operations
to complete the overall task.

00:01:53.830 --> 00:01:56.380
Reliably combining
these services

00:01:56.380 --> 00:01:59.440
requires re-tries with
exponential backup,

00:01:59.440 --> 00:02:01.810
which further
increases the runtime

00:02:01.810 --> 00:02:04.570
in worst-case scenarios.

00:02:04.570 --> 00:02:06.070
And then there's humans.

00:02:06.070 --> 00:02:07.870
They're wonderful, really.

00:02:07.870 --> 00:02:11.290
They can make pizzas and
authorize insurance claims.

00:02:11.290 --> 00:02:14.200
But they're rarely
as fast as computers.

00:02:14.200 --> 00:02:17.950
They often take days or
months to complete tasks.

00:02:17.950 --> 00:02:19.780
I know Guillaume
had to wait longer

00:02:19.780 --> 00:02:21.700
than he would have
liked for me to finish

00:02:21.700 --> 00:02:24.040
my part of this session.

00:02:24.040 --> 00:02:28.660
All of these factors contribute
to variable and long run times.

00:02:28.660 --> 00:02:31.690
If any of them
apply, your UI needs

00:02:31.690 --> 00:02:34.930
to take into account the
period of time the operation is

00:02:34.930 --> 00:02:38.710
in progress rather than assuming
all operations are quick

00:02:38.710 --> 00:02:41.260
and immediately successful.

00:02:41.260 --> 00:02:43.780
Now, while an
operation is running,

00:02:43.780 --> 00:02:46.930
a client UI might lose
its network connection,

00:02:46.930 --> 00:02:49.540
crash, or simply be closed.

00:02:49.540 --> 00:02:53.830
If the operation depends on
an HTTP request staying alive,

00:02:53.830 --> 00:02:58.120
the real world will make
these operations unreliable.

00:02:58.120 --> 00:03:01.330
You need your operation
to continue asynchronously

00:03:01.330 --> 00:03:04.210
in the background,
running on your back end

00:03:04.210 --> 00:03:08.360
regardless of what
happens to the front end.

00:03:08.360 --> 00:03:12.530
So back to our user, who
asked our app to do something.

00:03:12.530 --> 00:03:15.650
They need to see real-time
feedback to trust

00:03:15.650 --> 00:03:17.030
that the app is working.

00:03:17.030 --> 00:03:21.230
Few things create a more
uncomfortable user experience

00:03:21.230 --> 00:03:24.650
than clicking a button and
having no idea if it worked,

00:03:24.650 --> 00:03:27.050
or is working, or failed.

00:03:27.050 --> 00:03:30.650
For multi-step processes,
seeing which step is current

00:03:30.650 --> 00:03:33.490
can also be important.

00:03:33.490 --> 00:03:38.020
Today we'll show how to
deliver this reliability

00:03:38.020 --> 00:03:39.430
and reactivity.

00:03:39.430 --> 00:03:43.240
Our pattern will use Workflows
to reliably perform the work

00:03:43.240 --> 00:03:46.690
and Firestore to
drive a reactive UI.

00:03:46.690 --> 00:03:48.880
Guillaume, over
to you to walk us

00:03:48.880 --> 00:03:51.192
through this helpful pattern.

00:03:51.192 --> 00:03:53.150
GUILLAUME LAFORE: Workflows
is a fully managed,

00:03:53.150 --> 00:03:56.440
scalable service that
can orchestrate any APIs,

00:03:56.440 --> 00:03:58.840
internal or external.

00:03:58.840 --> 00:04:01.330
With Workflows, you can
externalize and monitor

00:04:01.330 --> 00:04:02.920
the logic of your
business processes

00:04:02.920 --> 00:04:04.690
and have it work
for you even when

00:04:04.690 --> 00:04:07.240
your mobile application
or website aren't

00:04:07.240 --> 00:04:09.750
running or active.

00:04:09.750 --> 00:04:13.440
The workflow is made up steps,
and you can go through them,

00:04:13.440 --> 00:04:16.470
loop through them, or
create different branches.

00:04:16.470 --> 00:04:19.589
Steps can even run in parallel.

00:04:19.589 --> 00:04:22.260
The two cool
aspects of Workflows

00:04:22.260 --> 00:04:25.410
are that you can create
callback endpoints

00:04:25.410 --> 00:04:29.700
that service can call back into
and that pauses your workflow.

00:04:29.700 --> 00:04:32.820
And when the endpoint
is called, it

00:04:32.820 --> 00:04:35.280
resumes the workflow execution.

00:04:35.280 --> 00:04:39.540
And secondly, Workflows can
even run a super-long time,

00:04:39.540 --> 00:04:41.230
even up to a year.

00:04:41.230 --> 00:04:44.640
So you can support a very
rich set of possible business

00:04:44.640 --> 00:04:46.720
workflows.

00:04:46.720 --> 00:04:49.690
For storing the status of our
long-running and asynchronous

00:04:49.690 --> 00:04:52.840
workflows, we decided to take
advantage of Google Cloud

00:04:52.840 --> 00:04:53.950
Firestore.

00:04:53.950 --> 00:04:57.340
Firestore is a great
NoSQL document database.

00:04:57.340 --> 00:04:59.710
It's serverless,
it's fully managed,

00:04:59.710 --> 00:05:02.770
so we don't have to worry about
the infrastructure management.

00:05:02.770 --> 00:05:04.720
And it scales transparently.

00:05:04.720 --> 00:05:07.360
It's also mobile-
and web-friendly.

00:05:07.360 --> 00:05:10.420
And in particular, thanks
to the Firebase integration,

00:05:10.420 --> 00:05:14.230
you can easily link
your UI to the data

00:05:14.230 --> 00:05:16.330
stored in the database.

00:05:16.330 --> 00:05:19.240
The icing on the cake is
the live synchronization

00:05:19.240 --> 00:05:21.340
and offline mode capabilities.

00:05:21.340 --> 00:05:24.940
In particular, in our use case
today, the live synchronization

00:05:24.940 --> 00:05:28.180
enables our UIs to
be really reactive

00:05:28.180 --> 00:05:32.970
and show changes as they
happen in the database.

00:05:32.970 --> 00:05:34.820
Let's see that in
action with an example

00:05:34.820 --> 00:05:37.250
showing a
pizza-ordering process,

00:05:37.250 --> 00:05:40.430
combining Workflows to
orchestrate the steps

00:05:40.430 --> 00:05:43.595
and Firestore to handle
real-time status updates.

00:05:46.400 --> 00:05:49.220
In this demo, we are going
to submit some order--

00:05:49.220 --> 00:05:51.740
for example, for a pizza.

00:05:51.740 --> 00:05:55.100
We have several steps
in our workflow.

00:05:55.100 --> 00:05:57.350
We received the
order; the payment

00:05:57.350 --> 00:06:00.620
is going to be processed;
the order is being prepared;

00:06:00.620 --> 00:06:04.080
then the order is shipped; and
at the end, it's delivered.

00:06:04.080 --> 00:06:07.610
So you are going to receive,
for example, your pizza.

00:06:07.610 --> 00:06:11.570
We're going to store
the state of our order

00:06:11.570 --> 00:06:13.800
in the Firestore database.

00:06:13.800 --> 00:06:16.640
So here I am seeing
the documents that

00:06:16.640 --> 00:06:17.960
are stored in the database.

00:06:17.960 --> 00:06:21.920
And at the bottom here, we
have the workflow execution.

00:06:21.920 --> 00:06:24.860
So there were a few
executions in the past.

00:06:24.860 --> 00:06:27.860
And we're going to see that
as soon as I submit the order,

00:06:27.860 --> 00:06:30.320
a new execution is created.

00:06:30.320 --> 00:06:35.390
And we'll have a look at the
status changes in the database.

00:06:35.390 --> 00:06:39.300
As soon as we go through one
of the steps of the workflow,

00:06:39.300 --> 00:06:42.000
we're going to update the
state in the database.

00:06:42.000 --> 00:06:47.420
And if you look at the UI, the
website, mobile application,

00:06:47.420 --> 00:06:49.760
et cetera will be
notified in real time

00:06:49.760 --> 00:06:52.940
that we went through a
new step in our workflow

00:06:52.940 --> 00:06:56.170
and we will update
the UI accordingly.

00:06:56.170 --> 00:06:57.570
So let's submit the order.

00:06:57.570 --> 00:07:00.670
I click on the Submit button.

00:07:00.670 --> 00:07:04.540
It is going to create
a new workflow here.

00:07:04.540 --> 00:07:06.660
So it's currently executing.

00:07:06.660 --> 00:07:10.230
And you can see
that at each step,

00:07:10.230 --> 00:07:12.930
the status is
changing-- prepared;

00:07:12.930 --> 00:07:14.910
now it's going to be shipped.

00:07:14.910 --> 00:07:16.650
As soon as rewriting
the database,

00:07:16.650 --> 00:07:20.460
we update the UI, turning
from gray into black

00:07:20.460 --> 00:07:22.950
and showing some ingredients.

00:07:22.950 --> 00:07:25.560
And at the end you see that
the workflow succeeded.

00:07:25.560 --> 00:07:31.160
We went through all the steps
of our workflow execution,

00:07:31.160 --> 00:07:36.660
with status changes
updating the UI live.

00:07:36.660 --> 00:07:40.560
To recap, when the user clicks
the button, the Submit button,

00:07:40.560 --> 00:07:43.920
the client-side JavaScript
calls the Google Cloud function

00:07:43.920 --> 00:07:46.770
that triggers the
execution of the workflow.

00:07:46.770 --> 00:07:49.590
For each key step
of the process,

00:07:49.590 --> 00:07:53.790
Workflows calls and
orchestrates various services

00:07:53.790 --> 00:07:57.090
to process the payment,
to prepare the order,

00:07:57.090 --> 00:07:58.920
ship the order, et cetera.

00:07:58.920 --> 00:08:03.300
And after each of these steps,
if they execute successfully,

00:08:03.300 --> 00:08:07.910
you update a status field
in Firestore for that order.

00:08:07.910 --> 00:08:10.760
Thanks to the Firebase SDK
running in the web page

00:08:10.760 --> 00:08:12.890
or mobile front
end, the front end

00:08:12.890 --> 00:08:16.980
is notified that a change has
been made to the database.

00:08:16.980 --> 00:08:19.400
Then the UI is
updated pretty much

00:08:19.400 --> 00:08:23.380
in real time with
the status change.

00:08:23.380 --> 00:08:25.690
On the client-side
JavaScript code,

00:08:25.690 --> 00:08:28.580
there are two key things
that we need to do.

00:08:28.580 --> 00:08:32.380
The first thing is to subscribe
to real-time notifications

00:08:32.380 --> 00:08:34.570
of the changes to
the document related

00:08:34.570 --> 00:08:37.720
to that particular
order in Firestore.

00:08:37.720 --> 00:08:40.179
When there's a change
in the status data,

00:08:40.179 --> 00:08:43.330
the onSnapshot event
handler is called,

00:08:43.330 --> 00:08:47.470
and we can update the
UI via some DOM changes.

00:08:47.470 --> 00:08:49.480
The other important
thing to do is

00:08:49.480 --> 00:08:52.750
to call the Cloud function,
thanks to the fetch method,

00:08:52.750 --> 00:08:58.300
to trigger the execution of
the order-processing workflow.

00:08:58.300 --> 00:09:01.960
The client-side JavaScript code
invokes this Cloud function,

00:09:01.960 --> 00:09:05.170
whose job is to trigger the
execution of the workflow.

00:09:05.170 --> 00:09:09.670
For that purpose, we use
Workflows Execution npm Module

00:09:09.670 --> 00:09:12.250
and its createExecution method.

00:09:12.250 --> 00:09:15.430
We pass the order ID as a
parameter to the workflow.

00:09:15.430 --> 00:09:18.430
But we should also send
the details of our order,

00:09:18.430 --> 00:09:22.060
with the ingredients of
the pizza, et cetera.

00:09:22.060 --> 00:09:25.090
Let's have a quick look at the
workflow definition showing

00:09:25.090 --> 00:09:28.490
the two operations we
combined for each step.

00:09:28.490 --> 00:09:31.390
First of all, in
this extract, a step

00:09:31.390 --> 00:09:34.150
calls the service that
prepares the order.

00:09:34.150 --> 00:09:37.900
And the following step updates
the status in Firestore.

00:09:37.900 --> 00:09:40.480
Interestingly, updating
a field in the document

00:09:40.480 --> 00:09:44.020
in the Firestore database is
really just a declarative goal

00:09:44.020 --> 00:09:45.670
in the workflow definition.

00:09:45.670 --> 00:09:49.540
No need to write actual
database handling code.

00:09:49.540 --> 00:09:54.130
We rely on Firestore's APIs
that Workflows can call

00:09:54.130 --> 00:09:56.920
in that declarative manner.

00:09:56.920 --> 00:09:59.710
Here we're actually
using a special connector

00:09:59.710 --> 00:10:00.700
for Firestore.

00:10:00.700 --> 00:10:03.880
We're not calling its REST
API manually directly.

00:10:03.880 --> 00:10:08.520
It simplifies the
usage of the API.

00:10:08.520 --> 00:10:10.320
We mentioned that
humans can be part

00:10:10.320 --> 00:10:12.690
of those long-running
processes and may

00:10:12.690 --> 00:10:17.580
need to validate, authorize,
give their go, no-go agreement.

00:10:17.580 --> 00:10:19.960
So how do we handle
those situations?

00:10:19.960 --> 00:10:23.790
That's where we'll benefit from
another Workflows features,

00:10:23.790 --> 00:10:26.280
HTTP callbacks.

00:10:26.280 --> 00:10:29.070
I'd like to show you another
demo, with a smart expense

00:10:29.070 --> 00:10:31.320
report application
where a manager needs

00:10:31.320 --> 00:10:35.250
to approve the expense
report sent by their team.

00:10:35.250 --> 00:10:38.340
In such an application, we
need someone to take a decision

00:10:38.340 --> 00:10:42.390
and approve or
reject the reports.

00:10:42.390 --> 00:10:45.390
Let's have a look at an
expense report application.

00:10:45.390 --> 00:10:47.980
We have two views.

00:10:47.980 --> 00:10:49.860
The first is the employee view.

00:10:49.860 --> 00:10:53.700
The employee is going
to submit receipts,

00:10:53.700 --> 00:10:57.900
and it's going to create
an expense report.

00:10:57.900 --> 00:10:59.970
The manager here on the right--

00:10:59.970 --> 00:11:03.180
we'll go into the
administration page--

00:11:03.180 --> 00:11:08.400
and the manager will wait
for incoming expense reports.

00:11:08.400 --> 00:11:13.380
The incoming reports will
be here in the queue,

00:11:13.380 --> 00:11:15.840
in the waiting-approval queue.

00:11:15.840 --> 00:11:17.850
On the left, the
employee is going

00:11:17.850 --> 00:11:20.400
to select a few
receipts-- so let's

00:11:20.400 --> 00:11:26.890
say, this one and
perhaps this one.

00:11:26.890 --> 00:11:29.660
The two receipts are selected.

00:11:29.660 --> 00:11:31.730
Now let's submit the report.

00:11:31.730 --> 00:11:34.120
So we're going to go
through different steps

00:11:34.120 --> 00:11:35.350
of our workflow.

00:11:35.350 --> 00:11:37.570
Submit the report.

00:11:37.570 --> 00:11:38.860
Receipts are added.

00:11:38.860 --> 00:11:40.450
Receipts are sent.

00:11:40.450 --> 00:11:42.440
The report is now
being processed.

00:11:42.440 --> 00:11:48.340
So we saw a couple or three
steps very fast in the UI,

00:11:48.340 --> 00:11:52.720
because the UI is updated
pretty much in real time

00:11:52.720 --> 00:11:55.450
as soon as there's a
status update after one

00:11:55.450 --> 00:11:57.020
of the steps of the workflow.

00:11:57.020 --> 00:11:59.230
So now the report
is being processed.

00:11:59.230 --> 00:12:03.490
We are using Document
AI to parse receipts.

00:12:03.490 --> 00:12:06.640
It's going to look at the
information in those receipts--

00:12:06.640 --> 00:12:12.220
all the line items, the amount
of each item, the total,

00:12:12.220 --> 00:12:16.750
the currency, also the
location where it's been--

00:12:16.750 --> 00:12:18.730
if it's a restaurant, et cetera.

00:12:18.730 --> 00:12:21.400
We'll have some
interesting information

00:12:21.400 --> 00:12:23.950
that the manager will
have to have a look at

00:12:23.950 --> 00:12:29.080
and say, OK, this is a
legit expense report.

00:12:29.080 --> 00:12:35.180
I will approve or reject
this expense report.

00:12:35.180 --> 00:12:37.390
So let's wait a
little bit while it's

00:12:37.390 --> 00:12:39.220
being processed by Document AI.

00:12:42.020 --> 00:12:44.180
Look, it appeared.

00:12:44.180 --> 00:12:48.510
So we now have the
expense report.

00:12:48.510 --> 00:12:53.100
Notice also there were
actually two real-time updates

00:12:53.100 --> 00:12:57.600
or changes in those two UIs.
There's the report that appears

00:12:57.600 --> 00:13:00.120
in the manager's
queue, and we also

00:13:00.120 --> 00:13:02.910
have the new state, the new
status here, saying report

00:13:02.910 --> 00:13:04.710
is awaiting validation.

00:13:04.710 --> 00:13:09.990
So now the workflow is
posed and is waiting

00:13:09.990 --> 00:13:12.210
for the manager's feedback.

00:13:12.210 --> 00:13:15.500
And for that purpose
we're going to use

00:13:15.500 --> 00:13:18.660
a great feature of Workflows,
Workflows Callbacks.

00:13:18.660 --> 00:13:24.780
So the UI for the manager is
going to call back the workflow

00:13:24.780 --> 00:13:26.400
to say, OK, here's my decision.

00:13:26.400 --> 00:13:30.930
I approve or I reject
this expense report.

00:13:30.930 --> 00:13:35.460
Document AI gave
useful information--

00:13:35.460 --> 00:13:39.720
the two restaurants, the
items, the total, the currency,

00:13:39.720 --> 00:13:40.290
et cetera.

00:13:40.290 --> 00:13:42.750
So we've got all the
right information.

00:13:42.750 --> 00:13:45.630
And, well, it seems
that my employee

00:13:45.630 --> 00:13:48.600
was traveling, so he had some
expenses at a restaurant.

00:13:48.600 --> 00:13:49.410
So that's great.

00:13:49.410 --> 00:13:52.440
I'm going to approve
the expense report.

00:13:52.440 --> 00:13:56.070
I approve it, and look-- it
disappears from my queue.

00:13:56.070 --> 00:13:59.610
And as an employee, I
see report validated.

00:13:59.610 --> 00:14:02.800
The manager has
validated my report.

00:14:02.800 --> 00:14:07.180
So I had status updates at
each step of the workflow.

00:14:07.180 --> 00:14:09.570
And the workflow
was posed, waiting

00:14:09.570 --> 00:14:13.290
for the validation or
rejection by the manager,

00:14:13.290 --> 00:14:16.800
using Workflows callback.

00:14:16.800 --> 00:14:19.140
Here's a recap of what's
happening under the hood.

00:14:19.140 --> 00:14:22.830
The employee uses the
website to store the receipts

00:14:22.830 --> 00:14:26.380
into Cloud Storage, thanks
to the Firebase SDK.

00:14:26.380 --> 00:14:28.840
The app triggers the
workflow execution

00:14:28.840 --> 00:14:30.850
via the Cloud function.

00:14:30.850 --> 00:14:34.420
The workflow calls the
Document AI service

00:14:34.420 --> 00:14:37.390
to figure out the
various line items

00:14:37.390 --> 00:14:40.810
of the receipts, the total
amount, the currency,

00:14:40.810 --> 00:14:42.940
the vendor, et cetera.

00:14:42.940 --> 00:14:45.430
All those receipt
details are stored

00:14:45.430 --> 00:14:47.050
in the Firestore database.

00:14:47.050 --> 00:14:49.870
Then in the management
view, the manager

00:14:49.870 --> 00:14:54.310
will see a new expense report
arrive, ready for their review.

00:14:54.310 --> 00:14:57.410
That's where the callback
system comes into play.

00:14:57.410 --> 00:15:00.070
The workflow is posed,
waiting for the decision

00:15:00.070 --> 00:15:01.180
of the manager.

00:15:01.180 --> 00:15:03.700
The manager sees all the
details about the expense

00:15:03.700 --> 00:15:09.490
report and its receipts, and
can then click Accept or Reject.

00:15:09.490 --> 00:15:12.500
When either of these
buttons is clicked,

00:15:12.500 --> 00:15:16.030
then the admin UI is
going to call the callback

00:15:16.030 --> 00:15:18.280
endpoint via another
Cloud function,

00:15:18.280 --> 00:15:21.760
and that will resume the
execution of the workflow.

00:15:21.760 --> 00:15:24.280
The acceptance or
rejection decision

00:15:24.280 --> 00:15:26.230
is stored in Firestore.

00:15:26.230 --> 00:15:30.250
And again, thanks to the
live synchronization feature,

00:15:30.250 --> 00:15:34.750
the UI will be updated live for
the employee at the same time,

00:15:34.750 --> 00:15:37.630
and they will see in the
report if the report was

00:15:37.630 --> 00:15:39.970
accepted or rejected.

00:15:39.970 --> 00:15:44.350
We, again, combined workflows
for reliably executing

00:15:44.350 --> 00:15:47.710
a process, even waiting
for a human intervention,

00:15:47.710 --> 00:15:50.920
updating the database, and
notifying all the stakeholders

00:15:50.920 --> 00:15:55.080
live as changes are made.

00:15:55.080 --> 00:15:57.960
Let me highlight the two key
steps in the workflow that take

00:15:57.960 --> 00:15:59.830
care of the callback mechanism.

00:15:59.830 --> 00:16:03.450
The first step here creates
a callback endpoint.

00:16:03.450 --> 00:16:06.720
So that means there
is a special URL that

00:16:06.720 --> 00:16:11.620
has been created that is going
to be open and be reachable

00:16:11.620 --> 00:16:14.460
so that some external service
can call back when it's ready,

00:16:14.460 --> 00:16:15.930
when it's done.

00:16:15.930 --> 00:16:18.510
Then the second step
is where we pause

00:16:18.510 --> 00:16:21.600
the execution of the
workflow to wait for the call

00:16:21.600 --> 00:16:23.220
to the callback endpoint.

00:16:23.220 --> 00:16:25.260
How do we take
advantage of this?

00:16:25.260 --> 00:16:27.750
Well, from the UI,
when the manager

00:16:27.750 --> 00:16:30.288
clicks on the Approve
or Reject button,

00:16:30.288 --> 00:16:31.830
this callback endpoint
is going to be

00:16:31.830 --> 00:16:36.000
called, via Cloud function, with
the outcome of the decision.

00:16:36.000 --> 00:16:40.490
And then the workflow
can resume its execution.

00:16:40.490 --> 00:16:43.520
Of course, depending on the
approval or rejection status,

00:16:43.520 --> 00:16:46.940
you can have different branches
of execution in your workflow

00:16:46.940 --> 00:16:50.330
and do further
steps conditionally.

00:16:50.330 --> 00:16:52.130
Although it's not
shown in these two

00:16:52.130 --> 00:16:54.980
extracts of the whole
workflow, at each key step

00:16:54.980 --> 00:16:57.830
of the process, we
are saving the status

00:16:57.830 --> 00:17:00.930
of the process in Firestore,
like in the previous demo,

00:17:00.930 --> 00:17:03.560
so that the UI
updates in real time

00:17:03.560 --> 00:17:05.690
as we progress
through the workflow

00:17:05.690 --> 00:17:08.130
and the various statuses.

00:17:08.130 --> 00:17:10.290
From the front end, we
call the Cloud function

00:17:10.290 --> 00:17:14.160
that will be responsible for
calling the callback endpoint.

00:17:14.160 --> 00:17:17.819
So here in this admin.js
JavaScript file,

00:17:17.819 --> 00:17:19.710
we are calling that
Cloud function,

00:17:19.710 --> 00:17:22.560
passing the callback
URL we want to reach,

00:17:22.560 --> 00:17:24.480
and also the approval status--

00:17:24.480 --> 00:17:29.210
if that expense report has
been accepted or rejected.

00:17:29.210 --> 00:17:31.080
In the Cloud function,
on the server side,

00:17:31.080 --> 00:17:34.220
we relay the call to the
Workflows callback endpoint.

00:17:34.220 --> 00:17:36.620
We need to authenticate to
call the callback endpoint;

00:17:36.620 --> 00:17:38.630
hence why we're
using the GoogleAuth

00:17:38.630 --> 00:17:41.240
library to get an access token.

00:17:41.240 --> 00:17:44.630
Then we call the actual URL
of the callback endpoint,

00:17:44.630 --> 00:17:48.560
passing the access token as
the bearer token and the status

00:17:48.560 --> 00:17:49.950
as payload.

00:17:49.950 --> 00:17:52.280
Now back at you, Kris,
for the last step

00:17:52.280 --> 00:17:55.340
of the workflow of our talk.

00:17:55.340 --> 00:17:56.840
KRIS BRAUN: What
Guillaume has shown

00:17:56.840 --> 00:18:00.380
us here is a very useful
pattern for trusted apps that

00:18:00.380 --> 00:18:02.720
can be applied widely.

00:18:02.720 --> 00:18:06.800
Workflows ensures resilience to
failures in your front-end app

00:18:06.800 --> 00:18:08.900
and back-end services.

00:18:08.900 --> 00:18:12.260
Operations that take time
for processing, retries,

00:18:12.260 --> 00:18:14.930
and actions are
easily incorporated.

00:18:14.930 --> 00:18:17.870
You can rest easy knowing
Workflows is keeping things

00:18:17.870 --> 00:18:19.670
running as expected.

00:18:19.670 --> 00:18:22.400
If any back-end services
fail, your workflow

00:18:22.400 --> 00:18:24.770
can specify exactly
what should happen,

00:18:24.770 --> 00:18:29.390
and you can observe the details
to remediate any failures.

00:18:29.390 --> 00:18:33.500
Firestore handles all the
magic required for your UI

00:18:33.500 --> 00:18:36.140
to display the real-time
status of the operation

00:18:36.140 --> 00:18:37.580
as it progresses.

00:18:37.580 --> 00:18:41.390
Operation status is current
even if the page is reloaded

00:18:41.390 --> 00:18:43.940
or the app is restarted.

00:18:43.940 --> 00:18:46.670
Together, Workflows
and Firestore

00:18:46.670 --> 00:18:49.340
deliver the reliability
and reactivity

00:18:49.340 --> 00:18:52.280
your app needs to
win users' trust.

00:18:52.280 --> 00:18:55.940
We look forward to seeing
what you'll empower your users

00:18:55.940 --> 00:18:58.990
to do through this pattern.

00:18:58.990 --> 00:19:00.400
Thank you for watching.

00:19:00.400 --> 00:19:02.860
Guillaume and I are
always happy to talk

00:19:02.860 --> 00:19:05.240
about serverless
application architecture,

00:19:05.240 --> 00:19:08.830
so please reach out with
any questions or ideas.

00:19:08.830 --> 00:19:10.720
Happy building.

00:19:10.720 --> 00:19:14.070
[ELECTRONIC MUSIC]

